(Discrepancy 1 — LANGUAGE.md:3158-3161 says all sinks share one name
 resolver and that special names like {arguments's first} "render
 identically whether the result is printed, written to a file, or built
 into a buffer". Under 0.4.7 printing and writing worked and building into
 a buffer SEGFAULTED: all three buffer sinks (set / copy / append) crashed
 on all three argument TEXT properties (first / last / all), with or
 without arguments passed, while the numeric ones ({arguments's count})
 and {current time's hour} were fine.
 **RESOLVED — fixed by vox #52 (0.4.8+).** The buffer sinks no longer
 crash; they copy the text, and the buffer prints what Print and write
 print. This probe was rewritten on 2026-08-21 to record the fixed
 behaviour across all three buffer verbs.
 One residue, and it is NOT this discrepancy: {arguments's all} is a LIST,
 and building it into a buffer now renders a raw pointer instead of
 crashing -- which is exactly the behaviour Discrepancy 2 already records
 for every non-Print sink. It is D2's finding, not a survival of D1, and
 D2 is still open.
 {arguments's last} is deliberately not in the assertions below: with no
 arguments it resolves to argv[0], which is the checker's temp path.
 Ran: VOX_CORE_PATH=/home/josj/scr/english/vox/coreasm /home/josj/scr/english/vox/target/release/vox D1.vox -o p && ./p
 (check-probes runs the binary with NO arguments, which is what the block
 below records: {arguments's first} is empty and the count is 1.)
 Run by hand as `./p hello there` and it prints instead:
   copy first: / hello / set first: / hello / append first: / hello /
   count into a buffer: / 3 / no crash.
 expected output:
   copy first:

   set first:

   append first:

   count into a buffer:
   1
   no crash.
)
print "copy first:".
a buffer called b1 is 64 bytes in size.
copy "{arguments's first}" to b1.
Print b1.
print "set first:".
a buffer called b2 is 64 bytes in size.
set b2 to "{arguments's first}".
Print b2.
print "append first:".
a buffer called b3 is 64 bytes in size.
append "{arguments's first}" to b3.
Print b3.
print "count into a buffer:".
a buffer called b4 is 64 bytes in size.
copy "{arguments's count}" to b4.
Print b4.
print "no crash.".
